\name{bmeas-frontend}
\alias{bmf}
\alias{bhmf}
\alias{bmeas}
\alias{bhmm}
\alias{component}
\alias{set_prior}
\alias{get_prior}
\alias{model_table}
\alias{check_model}
\alias{stancode}
\alias{standata}
\alias{posterior_summary}
\title{Experimental component-first measurement declarations}
\description{Construct and inspect component-first measurement declarations with ordinary fixed/equal loadings, fixed-basis or share expressions, optional marker component SDs, parameter residuals, and task/fixed observation operands. The unified generator has bounded fixed/one-label correlation, marker and unit-SD replay evidence; supported unqualified compositions require explicit experimental execution. Fixed-state arithmetic does not establish identification, recovery or HMC validation.}

\usage{
bmf(..., cor = "free", components = NULL, residual = NULL,
      scale = NULL, loadings = NULL, decomposition = NULL, person = "iid", augmentation = NULL)
component(targets, basis, loading)
bmeas(formula, data = NULL, subject = NULL, family = NULL, subjects = NULL,
     prior = NULL, missing = "error", fit = TRUE, chains = NULL,
     iter = NULL, warmup = NULL, cores = 1L, threads = 1L,
     seed = 1L, grainsize = 1L, cache_dir = "bhmm-cache", output_dir = "bhmm-draws",
     control = list(), save_log_lik = FALSE, exploratory = FALSE,
     execution = "qualified", resources = NULL)
set_prior(prior, class, component = NULL, target = NULL, layer = "component",
          decomposition = NULL, stratum = NULL, term = NULL, coordinate = NULL, block = NULL)
get_prior(object)
model_table(object, table = NULL)
check_model(object)
stancode(object, file = NULL, threads = 1L, log_lik = FALSE)
standata(object, threads = 1L, reduction = "serial", grainsize = 1L)
posterior_summary(object)
}
\arguments{
\item{...}{Named one-sided component formulas. RHS targets are literal wide Gaussian columns or \code{block$parameter[task_selector]}. Arithmetic and arbitrary calls are rejected.}
\item{cor}{Free or independent component correlations, or a named list with a zero/free default and explicit zero/free formula lists. Fixed rules use a scalar pairs formula and numeric value; named equal classes contain at least two distinct scalar-pair formulas. Fully fixed strict-PD and one-label affine layers support code generation and an explicit experimental iid/common-plus-residual runtime. Separate sample-centered/task-Cholesky profiles support either ordered raw common coordinates or an ordinary zero/free final-marginal common MGS graph, with bounded affine residual layers and explicit experimental execution. An explicit experimental iid canonical-Gaussian common and/or residual-product profile supports zero-separated one-label blocks, including shared aliases across blocks. Product saved export uses a separate bounded profile; general connected multilabel charts remain unavailable. Constraints refer to final marginal correlations. Optional coordinates can specify type="ordered_blocks" and an ordered named blocks list, with explicit raw-B priors.}
\item{components}{Alternative named list for component names colliding with argument names.}
\item{residual}{NULL omits parameter residuals. A list with targets as a one-sided target formula and optional cor declares person-target residual effects. Its cor defaults to independent and uses the same zero/free/fixed/equal grammar with target selectors; fixed/one-label affine residual maps use the same bounded source/runtime policy. Common components may be empty for a direct target-covariance model.}
\item{targets, basis, loading}{For component(), a captured one-sided target formula and a plain named finite real numeric matrix. The loading mode must be explicit: shared supplies one positive amplitude on surviving unowned rows; decomposition requires every nonzero row to be owned by a variance-share declaration. Rows match target names, columns name expanded unit coordinates, and no normalization is performed.}
\item{loadings}{Optional exact fixed/equal rules for declared ordinary scalar cells. Fixed rules contain value and cells; named equality classes contain component-to-target-selector maps. Derived basis/share cells cannot be constrained. The same affine compiler uses one parameter/prior per free class and exact constants for fixed cells; execution retains bounded qualification or explicit experimental policy.}
\item{person}{Default iid, or an explicit sample-centered roster declared as list(distribution="sample_centered", ids=ordered_public_ids). The centered mode requires task_cholesky augmentation. Together with task augmentation and either ordered raw-B coordinates or an effective ordinary zero/free final-marginal common graph it has an explicitly experimental runtime profile under an explicit resource policy.}
\item{augmentation}{Optional list(type="task_cholesky", targets=one_sided_formula). Targets are the exact ordered symbolic active domain; standalone targets=~0 denotes an empty domain. The conjunction with a centered roster and either ordered raw-B coordinates or an effective ordinary zero/free final-marginal common graph can request explicit experimental execution; genuine fixed/equal common affine constraints and other combinations retain separate R/source support.}
\item{block}{Destination-block selector for one zero-centered Normal prior per cor_raw primitive.}
\item{scale}{ NULL or an empty scale list keeps unit component SDs. An explicit component entry such as scale=list(G=list(marker=~x1,value=1)) fixes that declared beta cell and adds one positive population component SD. Negative finite nonzero marker values are legal; an omitted value is one. Marked columns must be entirely ordinary scalar loadings, with no basis/share-derived cells.}
\item{decomposition}{For bmf, a named list of variance-share owners with explicit type, targets, whole component groups, residual flag and target-level shares design. For set_prior, an optional owner-name selector for total_sd or share roles.}
\item{stratum, term, coordinate}{Share-prior selectors for active-set IDs, design-column names and contrast-column names; explicit vectors select independent primitive coefficients.}
\item{formula}{A bmf declaration or already prepared bmeas spec.}
\item{data}{A wide numeric-response data frame, or named observe() blocks. Blocks may capture explicit parameter variation levels; role and level remain separate.}
\item{subject}{Subject column for wide data.}
\item{family}{Only Gaussian identity is supported in the wide path; explicit blocks carry their own contracts.}
\item{subjects}{Shared, separate, or explicit crosswalk for multiple blocks.}
\item{prior}{For set_prior, a safely parsed distribution string. For bmeas, combined set_prior objects; missing custom priors remain inspectable.}
\item{missing}{Error or observed_only response policy.}
\item{fit}{FALSE prepares a frozen inspectable context without compilation or sampling. TRUE requires explicitly supplied sampling counts, an explicit finite resources policy and a supported composition with a qualified or explicitly experimental execution profile.}
\item{resources}{An explicit plain finite policy with max_output_bytes and cache, or the named development preset. NULL permits main planning with a policy-required result but refuses fitting before backend work. Deferred cache avoids posterior materialization; caller eager policy additionally requires max_cache_draw_values and remains refused when its full provider-wide schedule cannot be certified. See execution_plan and RESOURCE_POLICY.md.}
\item{chains, iter, warmup, cores, threads, seed, grainsize}{Explicit finite execution controls. Iter includes warmup; the requested run is checked against its resource policy. The named development preset retains historical small-run limits; mathematical/profile guards remain independent.}
\item{cache_dir, output_dir}{Compilation cache and sampler output locations; no files are generated by preparation.}
\item{control}{A list containing supported max_treedepth, adapt_delta and csv_sig_figs controls. csv_sig_figs is an integer from 1 through 18, defaulting to 17 for new main-API fits. Historical output retains its recorded precision. The numeric CSV formatting estimate reserves 32 bytes per saved scalar field through 12 digits and 40 above 12; this is a heuristic, not an exact file-size or process-memory bound.}
\item{save_log_lik}{Explicitly retain normalized pointwise density output during a fit; FALSE by default. No LOO is performed.}
\item{exploratory}{Explicitly allow a supported run when numerical local-rank checks are unresolved. FALSE blocks such fitting before backend work. This never overrides proven nonidentification; the warning and override remain in cached results.}
\item{execution}{Qualified (default), or explicit experimental. Canonical M3-CS plus SDM component, residual and variance-share models outside the narrow direct control require the experimental profile; it does not establish identification or bypass hard nonidentification/budget failures. A canonical builtin or explicitly registered typed custom row adapter can use the same compiler under this opt-in; unsupported contracts remain blocked. Inspect backend support separately from exact-composition qualification. The actual used profile is cached separately from the requested value.}
\item{class}{Intercept, loading, amplitude, sigma, sdlog, observation_sd, parameter_sd, component_sd, total_sd, share, cor_raw or cor prior role. Cor is one joint density; cor_raw assigns a zero-centered Normal density once per selected raw-B coordinate.}
\item{component, target}{Optional prior selectors. Component is a plain component-group name for ordinary loading or shared-amplitude priors. Amplitudes reject target selectors. Target accepts a canonical scalar key or a one-sided captured target formula. Qualified selectors are literal parameter roles; Gaussian-wide indicator names resolve their mean or observation-sigma role from class. Every group member must match a selected free coefficient. Parameter SD accepts targets but no component selector.}
\item{layer}{For cor priors only, component (default) or parameter selects the corresponding joint correlation matrix. Other prior roles already determine their layer.}
\item{object}{Prepared bmeas spec, or a fit, portable fit, or retained-snapshot archive handle for posterior_summary. get_prior also accepts a historical measurement spec.}
\item{table}{One table name, or NULL for all tables.}
\item{file}{Optional Stan source output filename.}
\item{log_lik}{Whether to explicitly include normalized row log likelihoods in generated quantities. No LOO is computed.}
\item{reduction}{Serial or reduce_sum_static.}
}
\details{The independent-block common and/or residual-product affine profile requires iid canonical Gaussian-only observations and explicit experimental execution. Each potentially nonzero connected block contains at most one unique label; a repeated label uses its full intersection interval and one Jacobian. Each layer retains its separate complete-layer determinant prior. Residual/dual cached-executable evidence covers five common-layer configurations; direct transformed-parameter GQ outputs were unavailable, and the saved-output comparator was corrected without native reruns or tolerance changes. Live cached reporting is bounded. A separate saved-value product profile admits portable/archive export, passive reports, component draws and individual summaries. Active PPC and diagnostic refresh remain closed for that profile. Deterministic native evidence is arithmetic only, not inference or scientific adequacy.


Centered/task models with ordinary final-marginal common zero/free graphs have an explicit experimental profile with residual MGS, fixed or one-label maps. Its separate deterministic gate covers 14 configurations, 412 primary derivatives, 264 normalized rows, 10684 scalar outputs and 194 empty fields, plus two same-state controls with 81 repeated derivatives. Common R uses a joint final-marginal density and joint MGS chart; no ordered raw-B prior is substituted. The existing historical structured-map rebuild boundary remains explicit.

The centered/task/ordered raw common plus bounded affine residual gate separately covers 14 configurations, 330 primary derivatives, 234 normalized rows, 7754 scalar outputs and 160 empty fields, plus two same-state controls with 58 repeated derivatives. Raw common R is derived; only free residual labels have marginal correlation priors. Generic identification, posterior propriety and sampling validation remain separate.

The iid affine arithmetic gate covers 32 configurations, 766 primary gradients, 594 normalized likelihood rows and10 exact earlier-state replays, plus two same-state threading checks. Both zero-unknown cases use fixed-parameter likelihood outputs only. No MCMC or recovery validation is claimed. Current binaries verified actual stanc --O0 and model-translation-unit C++ -O0; linked dependencies remain a separate toolchain scope.

Explicit sample-centered rosters, task-Cholesky augmentation and ordered raw-B coordinates share the same component compiler. Their conjunction has a bounded experimental runtime profile, with retained20-configuration/1794-gradient evidence and no MCMC claim. Other mode combinations do not inherit that profile. The former tiny caps are an explicit development preset; a separate finite caller policy controls main fitting resources. Task summaries distinguish N-1 white basis coordinates, N centered white scores and task deviations; common/residual generative scores are unsampled. Raw-B priors are primitive and final R is derived. Completely fixed models refuse sampling with E_NO_FREE_PARAMETERS, while free means/noise with no active person effects remain distinct.

A controlled self-authored Normal adapter plus two Gaussian blocks was checked at two fixed states (44 analytic gradients and400 generated values), including serial/reduced agreement. This evidence is separate from arbitrary custom or three-family execution and adds no MCMC. Backend support is derived from validated scientific contracts and resolved operand roles, independently of display names. Multiple basic blocks and registered custom densities require explicit experimental execution unless a narrower qualified profile matches. Registration is not scientific or security certification. The current hard prior, identification, propriety, source, type and resource gates remain in force on cache hits.


Marker-scale lowering passed24 fixed states,438 named gradients and4088 generated values, including signed markers, unique SD priors, old Gaussian replay and the full U6 cognitive direct replay. The maximum independent discrepancy was4.53e-9. Gaussian execution retains its bounded profile and other supported compositions require explicit experimental execution. Earlier prepared contexts must be explicitly rebuilt under the current reference-law schema.

The initialization record in check_model uses resolved prior scales for marked SDs and the prior center for signed marked beta. Other finite legacy starts are retained, with prior-based fallbacks where needed. Primitive support, inverse transforms, individual and aggregate prior arithmetic and typed operands are screened before backend work. The actual execution-time recipe, primitive table and constrained-init hash persist in fit provenance and the detached cache. E_INITIALIZATION_NUMERICAL blocks an unsupported start while preserving inspection and code generation. Optional covariance exports need not be representable for a finite effective-loading start. This screen does not qualify arbitrary likelihood arithmetic or extreme-scale HMC.

Caches distinguish beta, effective loadings, component SD, standardized z, scaled f and component covariance. Optional unavailable exports preserve draws and explicit flags; exact zero values can have legitimate negative-infinite log magnitudes.

Declared Lambda remains beta. Effective loadings are A=beta times component SD by column; predictors use A times standardized z. Scaled scientific scores f=SD*z, component covariance diag(SD)*R*diag(SD), and standardized unit variance have separate labels. Fixed beta has no loading prior or free coordinate, but its effective loading is uncertain if the component SD is free. Exactly one component_sd prior is allocated per explicitly marked component, including markers tied into the same fixed class. Priors accept positive half-Normal or zero-location half-Student-t and component selectors, never target selectors.

Marker defaults use the marker target calibration a_m divided by absolute marker c for its half-Normal SD scale, and divide each remaining beta proposal by that default SD scale. They are parameterization-specific independent priors, not a transport of the original unit-SD joint loading prior. Every proposal/source remains inspectable; unavailable or unrepresentable defaults remain missing until valid explicit priors are supplied. A user SD prior does not change the beta calibration denominator. Nonrepresentable actual effective loadings fail without clipping. Separately unrepresentable component-covariance exports return explicit unavailable entries/status while representable effective covariance remains inspectable.

The variance-share increment has inspectable preparation, tables, priors, covariance diagnostics and native source/data lowering. The changed generator passed a separate bounded deterministic gate, including no-owner replay. Gaussian execution retains engineering-only qualification; other canonical cognitive compositions require explicit experimental execution. This does not establish scientific or HMC validity. Owners replace selected loading and residual-SD expressions by total_sd times square-root share times a supplied unit-norm row. One-part simplexes have no share coefficient. Required owner-local marginal zeros override background defaults, conflict with explicit free edges and never create a transitive closure. Target-level designs and contrast matrices are captured without normalization; shares=~1 gives an independent coefficient block for each explicitly separated active-part set. Generic iid/final-R priors differ from the original centered/raw-cross-block reference profile.

Share priors are Normal densities on unique logit-contrast coefficients, with no softmax Jacobian. Total-SD priors are positive half-Normal or half-Student-t. Consumed loadings/amplitudes/residual SDs receive no duplicate prior. An owned positive scalar loading sets orientation globally; remaining unowned loadings stay signed. Residual-only one-part scales and genuinely free residual/common logit directions retain dependency-aware Gaussian noise-confounding checks; fixed weights or shared designs are assessed through their actual covariance map.

Under default iid/common-plus-residual augmentation, standardized components have unit marginal variance and iid person scores. Explicit markers can give scientific components free population SDs. Unconstrained ordinary loading cells are separate unknowns; declared equality classes share one primitive and one prior, while fixed classes have no primitive. A calibrated free class uses the maximum of all valid member scales, with every proposal visible. Identical canonical maximal user densities are idempotent; conflicting densities error. Fixed/equal loading constraints couple sign gauges, so one positive anchor is chosen per unpinned connected group when that group representation applies. New fixed/equal marginal correlation constraints require a general GF(2) basis and independent anchor characters instead. Pinned coordinates retain signed free coefficients. The historical ordinary-loading affine native gate covers24 fixed states,368 gradients and2494 generated values, with20 previous-runtime comparisons and no MCMC. The crossed two-class arithmetic fixture has a known column-permutation alias and is rejected by a scoped global-identification proof. This is bounded numerical evidence, not general identification or inference validation. Fixed basis cells share one positive amplitude per group and have no separate loading priors. Supplied basis values, signs, rank-deficient columns and explicit zero rows are retained. One amplitude prior is required per basis group. The orientation table exposes fixed/derived pins, permitted simultaneous flips and the selected unique primitive anchor under symmetric loading and correlation priors. Overlaps add loadings, not likelihood rows. Missing loading cells are exactly zero. Gaussian observation noise is retained. Optional parameter residuals add u to alpha plus Lambda times z: u has its own positive target SDs and marginal correlation matrix and is independent of the common z layer. The same person-target u is shared across repeated observations; observation noise is drawn separately per row. A sparse residual declaration creates no SD or residual coordinate for omitted targets. Direct covariance is the common-empty case, without scientific components invented for each target.

Grouped prior rules assign independent densities to unique coefficients, never equality constraints. All rules are resolved together. An explicit target/component restriction refines an unrestricted one; a target subset refines its superset. Equivalent or incomparable overlaps are errors unless one uniquely more-specific rule resolves each affected coefficient. List order does not set precedence. Even a restriction covering every current target remains more specific than an unrestricted predicate.

Task-vector names are looked up and copied at declaration. Active bindings and classed character selector objects are rejected before their functions/coercions can run. Normal R lazy arguments can still be forced while obtaining supplied values; ordinary argument expressions use normal R semantics. The formula AST itself is parsed symbolically and unsupported inline calls are rejected. This is not an arbitrary-code security boundary.

Ordinary Gaussian defaults are explicitly data-calibrated: median location, MAD scale with sample-SD then one-unit fallback, Student-t intercepts, zero-centered normal loadings and half-Student-t observation scales. Lognormal defaults use log responses; binomial predictors use fixed logit-scale defaults. Custom likelihoods without profiles leave missing priors. Calibration inputs and source rows are attached to the prior table. Under the existing zero/free final-marginal backend, each free correlation layer has one joint density and one reduced chart Jacobian; different eta values are allowed. The joint correlation profile is proportional to determinant(R) raised to eta minus one on the unique free marginal coordinates; its default eta is two, which favors the interior relative to the flat eta-one profile. This is not one prior per edge. Values below one remain declarations and are not execution-qualified by this numerical implementation; they are never silently changed. For new fixed/equal affine maps, the same spelling is a restricted determinant kernel on unique-label Lebesgue measure, whose normalizer depends on the fixed constraints and eta. Prior normalizability may be proven proper, proven improper or unassessed depending on this geometry and is separate from posterior propriety. Fully fixed and one-label affine code generation is available when the full domain is numerically resolved. New affine execution requires explicit experimental mode, eta at least one and all usual scientific/resource guards; sample-centered/task-Cholesky/ordered raw common coordinates with a separate fixed or one-label affine residual layer have their own explicit experimental profile. Centered/task ordinary zero/free final-marginal common MGS graphs also support these residual layers under their separate experimental profile. Other legal raw/centered/task affine combinations remain source-only, and raw B cannot share its common matrix with affine constraints. Fully fixed layers have no correlation prior or dummy primitive. A one-label layer uses one full-interval transform and one Jacobian, independently of alias multiplicity. Primitive summaries use one scientific rho row per unique label; expanded matrix aliases and fixed constants are reported separately.

For supported iid Gaussian models, checks count observable covariance moments under actual missingness and examine local Jacobian ranks. Negative degrees of freedom proves nonidentification in this restricted class. A rank-aware latent-covariance dimension bound can also prove an intrinsic decomposition failure; a nonnegative bound is not sufficient. Full local rank is not global identification. For existing zero/free residual graphs with separate free Gaussian observation and parameter SDs, a target without within-person replication has an exact noise-decomposition ambiguity. New fixed/equal residual constraints may invalidate that transformation and instead use the actual constrained moment map: a known nonzero residual triangle can be identified without repeats, and one repeat can identify a shared-rho triangle. Information is aggregated across all members of a unique correlation label. One missing-overlap edge cannot invalidate a label informed through another member. Cognitive identification otherwise remains unassessed. Known zero-trial/count likelihood units are retained in data but do not create information overlap. Both missing priors and identification failures remain inspectable; fitting gates reject them.

Gaussian diagnostic certificates are preserved under parameter-independent fixed factors. The gaussian_diagnostics record distinguishes trusted positive binomial/empty factors from unevaluated opaque constants. An opaque fixed factor cannot normalize a certified divergent Gaussian factor; that case reports proven_non_normalizable and stops before backend work without certifying the opaque density. Structurally constant covariance entries do not inflate Gaussian moment counts. All original likelihood rows and the actual mixed execution profile remain unchanged.

A separate exact-data posterior-propriety screen detects sufficient Gaussian constant-target and perfect-within-person-repeat collapse conditions under the implemented free-scale priors. These proven failures block execution without changing observations or priors; earlier structural and invalid-prior gates have precedence. Negative screening is not a general propriety proof. Data-calibration overflow is an inspectable invalid prior, not silently capped.

Prepared contexts from older schemas require an explicit rebuild from the saved declaration, original observation data and intended priors. Calling bmeas(old_spec, fit=FALSE) does not migrate an incompatible prepared context. Supported older formula captures can be used in a new public construction with formula provenance. Unknown/incomplete schemas raise E_SCHEMA_VERSION; a changed cross-package serialization hash is never repaired or resealed. Cached summaries remain historical records and do not migrate.

The default final-marginal chart uses a positive triangular factor from two-pass modified Gram-Schmidt and constructs the declared marginal matrix before Cholesky. It has explicit local numerical-failure checks without clipping or jitter; these do not prove full support in floating-point arithmetic. The fit cache separately screens stored-matrix conditioning and preserves observed numerical-failure console messages. Such counts may be lower bounds. Known reference and registered validation callbacks are eagerly byte-compiled; stable primitive validators are retained unchanged. Serialization-unstable callback environments fail with E_CALLBACK_LIFECYCLE before preparation. Stable serialization does not freeze mutable global or namespace dependencies or stateful behavior: these remain caller-managed, outside the frozen-context reproducibility guarantee, and are reported for custom callbacks. Explicit stanc O0 is part of compiler/cache identity: the installed stanc O1 was observed to miscompile an early return inside the original SDM search loop. No reference likelihood was rewritten. Summary and posterior_summary read caches only. No uniform posterior-region cutoff is silently introduced.}
\value{bmf returns a declaration; bmeas with fit=FALSE returns a prepared object with bmeas_spec plus the legacy bhmm_spec class; an explicitly permitted fit returns bmeas_fit plus the legacy bhmm_fit class with cached summaries. Inspection returns tables or status lists. set_prior returns a combinable prior declaration. Unsupported capabilities raise typed errors.}
\examples{
d <- data.frame(person = 1:6, x1 = 1:6, x2 = c(2,1,4,3,6,5),
                x3 = c(3,1,2,6,4,5))
m <- bmf(Ability = ~ x1 + x2 + x3)
s <- bmeas(m, data = d, subject = "person", fit = FALSE)
model_table(s, "loadings")
check_model(s)$identification$status
get_prior(s)
p <- c(set_prior("normal(0,1)", "loading"),
       set_prior("normal(0,.5)", "loading", target = ~ x1 + x2 + x3),
       set_prior("normal(0,.2)", "loading", target = ~ x1))
sp <- bmeas(m, d, subject = "person", prior = p, fit = FALSE)
get_prior(sp)
# Two rows per person distinguish parameter residuals from observation noise.
repeated <- rbind(d, transform(d, x1=x1+.2, x2=x2-.1, x3=x3+.15))
md <- bmf(residual = list(targets = ~ x1 + x2 + x3, cor = "free"))
sd <- bmeas(md, repeated, subject = "person", fit = FALSE)
model_table(sd, "parameter_residuals")
get_prior(sd)
Q <- matrix(c(-1,0,1), 3, 1,
            dimnames=list(c("x1","x2","x3"), "linear"))
mb <- bmf(G=~x1+x2+x3,
           P=component(~x1+x2+x3, Q, loading="shared"),
           cor="independent")
sb <- bmeas(mb, repeated, subject="person", fit=FALSE,
           prior=set_prior("half_normal(.5)", "amplitude", component="P"))
model_table(sb, "loading_coefficients")
}
